{
  "concept": "Every line's equation y = mx + b is readable straight off its picture: the slope m controls direction and steepness, the intercept b controls where it crosses the y-axis, and together they uniquely identify which drawn line is which.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Cartesian grid with light gray unit squares covering x from -7 to 7 and y from -5 to 5",
    "Horizontal x-axis with arrowheads on both ends, tick numbers -7 through 7, italic label 'x' at right end",
    "Vertical y-axis with arrowheads on both ends, tick numbers -5 through 5, italic label 'y' at top",
    "Line I — orange, steepest positive slope (about 4.5), crosses y-axis near y = 1, runs from about (-1.3, -4.6) to (0.9, 5), double arrowheads, label 'I' in orange just above its upper end",
    "Line II — light blue, shallowest positive slope (about 1/2), crosses x-axis near x = -6.2 and y-axis at y = 3, ends near (4, 5), double arrowheads, label 'II' in light blue at upper right end",
    "Line III — teal, positive slope about 2, crosses y-axis at y = -3 and x-axis near x = 1.5, rises to about (4, 5), double arrowheads, label 'III' in teal at upper right end",
    "Line IV — dark blue, the only negative slope (about -2), crosses y-axis at y = 3, descends to about (4, -4.5), double arrowheads, label 'IV' in dark blue at lower right end",
    "Movable dashed 'candidate line' in magenta drawn from the learner's m and b, with a small dot marking its y-intercept",
    "Live equation readout y = m x + b for the candidate line",
    "Rise-over-run step markers (dashed vertical 'rise' and horizontal 'run' segments with numeric labels) shown on whichever numbered line is clicked",
    "Legend/label chips pairing color with Roman numeral"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "candidateSlope",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Slope sign flips the line's direction and slope magnitude controls how steep it looks, so steepness alone narrows the match to one or two of lines I–IV.",
      "label": "Slope m of candidate line",
      "range": [-5, 5, 0.25],
      "default": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "candidateIntercept",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "b slides the whole line vertically without changing its tilt, so two lines that rise at similar rates are told apart by where they cut the y-axis.",
      "label": "y-intercept b of candidate line",
      "range": [-5, 5, 0.5],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "selectLine",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking a numbered line shows its rise-over-run staircase, turning visual steepness into the actual numeric slope of its equation."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Four lines, four equations",
      "narration": "Four lines share one grid, each tagged with a Roman numeral. Only two numbers per line — its tilt and its y-crossing — are needed to name its equation.",
      "state": { "candidateSlope": 1, "candidateIntercept": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sign of the slope",
      "narration": "Drag the slope slider negative. The magenta candidate tips downhill and now leans the same way as line IV — the only falling line in the figure.",
      "state": { "candidateSlope": -2, "candidateIntercept": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lift it onto line IV",
      "narration": "Raise b to 3 and the candidate lands exactly on line IV. Same slope, same intercept, same line: y = -2x + 3.",
      "state": { "candidateSlope": -2, "candidateIntercept": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same intercept, different steepness",
      "narration": "Keep b at 3 and set the slope to 1/2. The candidate still crosses at 3 but now sweeps gently across to the left edge — that is line II, y = (1/2)x + 3.",
      "state": { "candidateSlope": 0.5, "candidateIntercept": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Steepness alone is not enough",
      "narration": "Set the slope to 2 with b still at 3 — the tilt is right for line III but the line sits too high. Lower b to -3 and it snaps onto line III, y = 2x - 3.",
      "state": { "candidateSlope": 2, "candidateIntercept": -3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The steepest one",
      "narration": "Crank the slope up past 4. The candidate becomes nearly vertical and matches line I, which climbs off the top of the grid almost immediately. Click any numbered line to see its rise-over-run confirmed.",
      "state": { "candidateSlope": 4.5, "candidateIntercept": 1 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A line is pinned down by exactly two readable facts — how fast it rises (m) and where it crosses the y-axis (b) — so matching equations to graphs is never guesswork.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square-ish coordinate plane with a pale gray unit grid, black axes with arrowheads at both ends, numbered -7…7 horizontally and -5…5 vertically, italic 'x' and 'y' axis labels. Four straight double-arrowed lines drawn across the full grid: orange line I, very steep positive slope near the y-axis with its label just right of the y-axis at the top; light blue line II, shallow rise from the left edge near x = -6 through (0,3) to the upper-right corner region; teal line III, slope about 2 from (0,-3) up through x-intercept near 1.5 to the upper right; dark blue line IV, the only descending line, from upper left through (0,3) down to about (4,-4.5). Roman numerals I–IV sit at the ends of their lines in matching colors. Overlay a magenta dashed candidate line controlled by the sliders, with a filled dot at its y-intercept and a small equation readout (y = mx + b with live values) in a corner. When a numbered line is clicked, thicken it, dim the others, and draw a dashed run-then-rise staircase between two lattice points on it labeled with the run, rise, and resulting slope fraction."
}

